<html>
<title>Debugging Existing Code - Example</title>
<body>


<h1>Debugging Existing Code - Example</h1>

<p>
This section gives a simple example of how to use the <strong>Direct Assembler</strong> to debug and change existing code. See the <a href="DirectMain.htm">Direct Assembler</a> section.
</p>

<p>
For this example, a small Studio II game written in CDP1802 code will be used.
</p>


<h2>Setting up</h2>

<p>
First set up the Studio II emulator with the Space Invaders game:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_invaders.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Computer</strong> selector - select RCA Studio II</li>
<li>(2) <strong>XML</strong> selector - select <tt>standard.xml</tt></li>
<li>(3) <strong>ROM @0400</strong> selector - select <tt>invaders.st2</tt></li>
<li>(4) <strong>Start</strong> button</li>
</ul>

<p>
A Studio II emulator running Space Invaders will start. Play the game enough so that most code gets executed: move in all directions, shoot some invaders, and get killed. By default, the keys are mapped as follows:
</p>
<ul>
<li>Left: arrow left (4 on keyboard A)</li>
<li>Right: arrow right (6 on keyboard A)</li>
<li>Fire: space (0 on keyboard B)</li>
<li>Restart: 0 or tab (0 on keyboard A)</li>
</ul>


<h2>Walking through the code</h2>

<p>
Select the <strong>Direct Assembler</strong> tab (1). This should show the <strong>Direct Assembler</strong> interface. Now change the address to 0424 (hexadecimal) by using the <strong>Move to:</strong> field (2) and/or the <strong>Navigation</strong> scrollbar (3). Address 0400 (hexadecimal) is the start address of all Studio II cartridge software.
</p>

<p>
This address will show an ST2 instruction <tt>SYS 45D</tt> (4) in <font color="#5C9597">steel blue</font>, which is a call to a CDP1802 code routine at address 045D (hexadecimal). From address 0402 (hexadecimal) to 041D (hexadecimal) (5) data bytes should be shown in <font color="#3947CE">blue</font>. From 041E (hexadecimal) onwards (6) CDP1802 code should be shown in black.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_start.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Direct Assembler</strong> tab</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Move to:</strong> field</li>
<li>(3) <strong>Navigation</strong> scrollbar</li>
<li>(4) <tt>SYS 45D</tt> instruction in <font color="#5C9597">steel blue</font></li>
<li>(5) Data bytes in <font color="#3947CE">blue</font> (addresses 0402-041D)</li>
<li>(6) CDP1802 code in black (from 041E onwards)</li>
</ul>

<p>
The Space Invaders game runs from 0400 (hexadecimal) to 07FF (hexadecimal). Do a manual walk through the code from the current address to 0800 (hexadecimal). The easiest way is to use <tt>SHIFT DOWN</tt>, which moves one page down on every press. Check that all code is shown as CDP1802 code in black/<font color="#EA6F32">orange</font> text and data is shown in <font color="#3947CE">blue</font>.
</p>

<p>
Pay close attention to all <font color="#3947CE">blue</font> text, as this is code or data that has not been executed by the emulator - it is either data or code that was not reached during the game.
</p>


<h2>Identifying unexecuted code</h2>

<p>
The following areas will probably show in <font color="#3947CE">blue</font> (depending on how long the game is played):
</p>
<ul>
<li>0402 (hexadecimal) - 041D (hexadecimal): At the start of the game this is likely some kind of table, but there is no proof yet.</li>
<li>0594 (hexadecimal) / 0598 (hexadecimal): Two bytes - the first is preceded by a <tt>SEX R3</tt> and both are followed by an <tt>OUT 2</tt>. This is clearly data; an <tt>OUT 2</tt> is being done with the value 04 stored at 0594 (hexadecimal):
<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_594_598.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
</li>
<li>0668 (hexadecimal): Same as 0594 (hexadecimal):
<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_668.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
</li>
<li>06CB (hexadecimal) - 06CF (hexadecimal): This must be code, as it is preceded by a <tt>BNZ D2</tt> - the zero case will execute 06CB (hexadecimal):
<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_6CB.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<p>
To manually correct this, set the mark type to <tt>1802</tt> (1) and select 06CB (hexadecimal) as the address (2). Press the <strong>Mark</strong> button (3) for every address. See the <a href="DirectMarking.htm">Memory Type Selection</a> section.</p>
</li>
<li>06E4 (hexadecimal) - 06E7 (hexadecimal): Same as above - mark as CDP1802 code via the <strong>Mark</strong> button.</li>
<li>0733 (hexadecimal) - 0737 (hexadecimal): Same as above - mark as CDP1802 code via the <strong>Mark</strong> button.</li>
<li>076A (hexadecimal): Same as 0594 (hexadecimal).</li>
<li>0770 (hexadecimal) - 07F7 (hexadecimal): Looks like the end of the code - probably not used.</li>
<li>07F8 (hexadecimal) - 07FF (hexadecimal): Maybe a table or checksum:
<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_end_code.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
</li>
</ul>


<h2>Setting up the program configuration</h2>

<p>
Now save the <a href="DirectConfiguration.htm">program configuration</a> including all debug information collected. Press the <strong>Configuration</strong> spinbutton (1) down so it shows configuration one, then fill in a file name (2) (e.g., <tt>invaders.bin</tt>), code start (3) to 0400 (hexadecimal), code end (4) to 07F7 (hexadecimal), end (5) to 07FF (hexadecimal), and press <strong>Store</strong> (6). The configuration should look as follows:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_conf.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<p>
Press <strong>Save</strong> (7) and give the configuration a name, e.g., <tt>invaders.config</tt>.
</p>

<p>
The reason for using 07F7 (hexadecimal) as code end is that the area from 07F8 (hexadecimal) to 07FF (hexadecimal) appears to be a table or checksum - not part of the code as such, but it still needs to be saved. Setting end to 07FF (hexadecimal) ensures the full range 0400 (hexadecimal) to 07FF (hexadecimal) is saved.
</p>


<h2>Checking location-specific code</h2>

<p>
Before making any changes to the code, check if any code is location-specific. Move to address 0402 (hexadecimal) and press <strong>Load V</strong>. This will list all locations that possibly reference address 0402 (hexadecimal). The following should be shown in the message area:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Other examples:</strong>
</p>
<pre>
Locations loading 'value' or 'location' 0402:
LD H-L: 047A
LD L:   04E3
LD H:   04FC
LD H:   050E
LD H:   0518
LD H:   05A5
LD H:   05B4
LD L:   06DD
LD L:   071D
</pre>

<ul>
<li>LD H-L: 047A (hexadecimal) - move to location 047A (hexadecimal). Here 0402 (hexadecimal) is loaded into RC (1). Looking closer, a few <tt>LDA RC</tt> instructions (2) are done, followed by a check for a 0 (3) that branches out of the routine. This confirms that 0402 (hexadecimal) to 041D (hexadecimal) is a data table ending with a 0 at 041D (hexadecimal).
<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_47A.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<p>
Mark 047A (hexadecimal) as an <tt>LDL</tt> instruction (select <tt>LDL</tt> instead of <tt>1802</tt> and press <strong>Mark</strong>), resulting in:
</p>
<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_47A_corrected.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
</li>
<li>LD L: 047D (hexadecimal) - part of the <tt>LDL</tt> found at 047A (hexadecimal).</li>
<li>LD L: 04E3 (hexadecimal) - at first glance this only loads 02 into RF.0. To verify, set a bookmark on address 04E3 (hexadecimal) and check what RF.1 is at this point. RF.1 = 08, confirming this is not an address within the code but points at RAM. No further action needed:
<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_4E3.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
</li>
<li>LD H: 04FC (hexadecimal) - starting at 04F9 (hexadecimal), RA is loaded with location 044A (hexadecimal), followed by a <tt>SEP A</tt>.
<p>
Replace this with an <tt>LDRL A,044A</tt> as the code loads RA.0 first and then RA.1 (select <tt>LDRL</tt> instead of <tt>1802</tt> and press <strong>Mark</strong>), resulting in:
</p>
<p>
<img src="images/assembler_example_4F9_corrected.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
</li>
<li>LD H: 050E (hexadecimal) - same as 04FC (hexadecimal); address 050B (hexadecimal) should be replaced by an <tt>LDRL R4,0437</tt>.</li>
<li>LD H: 0518 (hexadecimal) - same as 04FC (hexadecimal); address 0515 (hexadecimal) should be replaced by an <tt>LDRL R4,041E</tt>.</li>
<li>LD H: 05A5 (hexadecimal) - same as 04FC (hexadecimal); address 05A2 (hexadecimal) should be replaced by an <tt>LDRL R4,041E</tt>.</li>
<li>LD H: 05B4 (hexadecimal) - same as 04FC (hexadecimal); address 05B1 (hexadecimal) should be replaced by an <tt>LDRL R4,0437</tt>.</li>
<li>LD L: 06DD (hexadecimal) - same as 04E3 (hexadecimal), no action needed.</li>
<li>LD L: 071D (hexadecimal) - same as 04E3 (hexadecimal), no action needed.</li>
</ul>

<p>
After this, print 0400 (hexadecimal) to 0800 (hexadecimal) to a disassembly file (via <strong>Dis</strong>, see the <a href="DirectDisassembler.htm">Disassembler</a> section) and search for all <tt>SEP</tt> instructions to make sure all are covered. No further instances should be found.
</p>


<h2>Remaining data locations</h2>

<ul>
<li>0770 (hexadecimal): Contains D8 (hexadecimal). Pressing <strong>Branch</strong> and <strong>Load V</strong> on this address finds no related code, so this is assumed to be unused.</li>
<li>07F8 (hexadecimal) - 07FF (hexadecimal): Two locations reference this table: 064D (hexadecimal) and 0687 (hexadecimal). This is not easily handled automatically in the <strong>Direct Assembler</strong>, so note these down as locations to be changed manually if the code is moved to another location.</li>
</ul>


<h2>Making changes</h2>

<p>
The analysis is complete. It is now possible to insert a byte anywhere between 041E and 07F7 (hexadecimal), and the code will still run correctly. Inserting on 041E (hexadecimal) and playing the game should work.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> Inserting will give a memory warning. To clear this, fill memory from 0770 to 07F7 (hexadecimal) with 0, as the <strong>Direct Assembler</strong> treats 0 as unused memory.
</p>

<p>
This code could now be moved, adapted, and even ported to another emulator (e.g., an Elf) fairly easily.
</p>
</body>
</html>
